      'Coleco built genuine Atari 2600-compatible hardware into the expansion module rather than attempting software emulation, which was far beyond what 1982 hardware could do convincingly, giving ColecoVision owners access to the enormous existing 2600 game library on day one. This was a shrewd competitive move against Atari itself, instantly making the ColecoVision, a newer and more capable console, backward-compatible with the market leader\'s catalog while also offering its own superior exclusive games like the excellent home port of Donkey Kong. Atari was reportedly furious and explored legal action, though the module\'s approach of licensing/replicating open hardware behavior rather than copying Atari\'s actual chips made the legal footing murkier than a straightforward copyright case. Coleco followed this strategy with other expansion modules, including one that turned the console into a driving-game controller setup, reflecting a broader company strategy of maximizing the base console\'s versatility through add-ons rather than just new standalone hardware.',
