      'CPS-2 boards encrypted their program ROMs and required a small battery-backed security module to decrypt code on the fly during operation, meaning that once the internal battery died, typically after some years, the board would fail entirely rather than continue running, a deliberate anti-piracy tradeoff Capcom accepted to combat rampant illegal ROM-copying in Asian arcade markets during the CPS-1 era. This created a real long-term preservation problem decades later, as many original CPS-2 boards have since gone permanently dark once their batteries expired, and hobbyists have developed specialized battery-replacement and decryption-recovery techniques to keep or restore surviving hardware functional. The scheme was largely effective at the time against casual board-cloning operations, even though determined, well-resourced pirates in some regions eventually found workarounds regardless. This encryption arms race is a specific, well-documented example of how mid-1990s arcade publishers treated bootlegging as a serious enough financial threat to justify hardware that would eventually self-destruct.',
