      'MVS cabinets came in one, two, four, and six-slot configurations, with the six-slot version letting arcade operators offer a rotating menu of games from a single cabinet and swap out underperforming titles without needing entirely new hardware, a genuinely efficient business model for operators managing limited floor space. This modularity is part of why the Neo Geo arcade ecosystem sustained such a long, prolific fighting-game library throughout the 1990s, since operators could keep cabinets fresh cheaply while SNK kept selling new cartridges into an existing installed hardware base. The home AES console used electrically similar but physically incompatible cartridges, meaning MVS and AES versions of the same game, while running near-identical code, required separately manufactured cartridge shells, a detail collectors track closely today given the significant price difference between the two formats. SNK\'s continued support of MVS hardware well into the 2000s made it one of the longest-lived arcade platforms in history, rivaling even Nintendo and Sega\'s most enduring systems.',
